---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  current="workflows"
  title="Workflows — Easley Consulting"
  description="Three systems I've built and shipped: an extraction pipeline with fidelity auditing for a 700-page reference work, a 370-book chapter-card enrichment system, and a cross-domain auth bridge."
>
  <section class="section section--tight intro">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Workflows</p>
      <h1>Three things I've built.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        All three are running. Each started as a problem I was personally stuck on, which is usually how the good ones start. Walkthroughs below. What they do, how they're wired, where they almost didn't work.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section case" id="case-1">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <p class="case__num">Case 01</p>
      <h2>The Encyclopaedia.</h2>

      <p class="case__lede">
        I wanted a real reference work on the history of Western medicine, Galen through the early 20th century, sourced from primary texts. Every claim had to trace back to a quote in a real book on a real page. Hand-extracting it was a decade of work I wasn't going to live to finish.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each book gets OCR'd locally with marker-pdf, with Mistral as the fallback for the difficult scans. An agent reads each chapter and pulls out claims: the verbatim snippet, the page number, the themes, the people and places named. A second pass tries to break the first one. Did the agent actually quote, or did it paraphrase and call it a quote? A third pass watches the synthesized pages for connector drift, language that sounds card-grounded but isn't.
      </p>

      <p>
        When any layer fails, a structured critique loop kicks in. Audit, critique, rewrite, re-audit, usually one to three cycles, then the page moves on. Pages stitch claims into prose with inline citations; the verbatim quote itself stays gated behind a separate evidence page, so I'm not reproducing large chunks of any copyrighted source on a public surface.
      </p>

      <p class="case__pull">
        The hard lesson was audit-first. The first extraction paraphrased about forty percent of the time and I didn't catch it until the corpus was already large. The fidelity loop took Layer 2 from 60.3 percent to 98.5 percent. If I started this over, I'd build the auditor before the extractor.
      </p>

      <p>
        Output: 15,000+ evidence cards, 700+ encyclopaedia pages, every claim auditable back to a primary source. The whole thing renders as a public wiki.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section case" id="case-2">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <p class="case__num">Case 02</p>
      <h2>The library, indexed.</h2>

      <p class="case__lede">
        I draw on a 370-book reference library. Each book has 20 to 100 chapters. I needed two things at once: a retrieval index the AI tools could ground their answers in, and chapter summaries I'd actually want to read.
      </p>

      <p>
        One workflow with three content profiles (medical, philosophical, herbal), not a new prompt for each book. DeepSeek V4 Flash does the classification. I tested the Pro tier and it was eight times the cost for marginal gains on this kind of work, so I stripped it out of the script the same day. Authority names get normalized against a cache built from existing curriculum, so "GAS," "general adaptation syndrome," and "stress response" all collapse to one entity instead of fragmenting the index.
      </p>

      <p>
        Long runs persist to disk and resume after interruption. Multi-day jobs that get killed don't lose state. Books where chapter detection misfires get diverted to a repair queue rather than corrupting the main pass. The bad ones get human attention without blocking the good ones.
      </p>

      <p>
        Output: searchable metadata across 7,000+ chapters. Both AI-retrieval summaries and dense human-readable ones per chapter. The index now grounds every AI tool I run.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section case" id="case-3">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <p class="case__num">Case 03</p>
      <h2>One login, two stacks.</h2>

      <p class="case__lede">
        HerbalMedicine.AI sells subscriptions through WooCommerce Memberships, which lives in WordPress. The chat product itself runs on Astro and Supabase. Asking one user to keep two logins straight is a non-starter.
      </p>

      <p>
        The fix is a small WordPress plugin that mints a short-lived JWT signed with the user's membership tier. It hands the token out as a "Launch the chat" link inside the member dashboard. The Astro app has a callback route that validates the JWT, checks the tier, and trades it for a Supabase session. Magic-link fallback for anyone who lost the WP session along the way.
      </p>

      <p>
        Shipped May 1, 2026. Zero double-login support tickets since.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section how">
    <div class="wrap wrap--narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">How I work</p>
      <h2>The shape of an engagement.</h2>

      <dl class="how-list">
        <dt>Discovery first.</dt>
        <dd>Before I write code I write a short document: what the system has to do, what the user has to experience, what failure looks like. Usually a week. Usually free.</dd>

        <dt>Two-week increments.</dt>
        <dd>At the end of each, something works. If it doesn't, we stop and find out why before adding more.</dd>

        <dt>Plain code that lasts.</dt>
        <dd>Plain frameworks, sensible defaults, no clever abstractions. Someone else should be able to read it in two years and know what it does.</dd>

        <dt>What's out of scope.</dt>
        <dd>High-volume support, marketing copy, generic CRUD apps, anything where speed matters more than craft. Better hands than mine exist for those.</dd>
      </dl>

      <p style="margin-top: var(--space-5);"><a class="cta" href="/inquire">Start a conversation</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .intro h1 { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }

  .case + .case { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule-soft); }

  .case h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3.5vw, 2.6rem);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
  }

  .case__num {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--accent);
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .case__lede {
    font-size: 1.18rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-style: italic;
    max-width: none;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .case p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    max-width: none;
  }

  .case__pull {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  .how {
    background: var(--bg-elev);
  }

  .how-list {
    margin: var(--space-4) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-3);
  }

  .how-list dt {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
  }

  .how-list dd {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
